package ui

templ Help() {
	@pageLayout("Help", "", false) {
		<main class="help-main">
			<article class="help-content">
				<h1>Using SeamGraph</h1>
				<p>Read module dependencies, inspect public seams, and see how your architecture changes.</p>
				<nav aria-label="Help topics" class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
					<a href="#code-explorer">Code explorer</a><a href="#graph-controls">Graph controls</a><a href="#reference-modes">Reference modes</a><a href="#comparisons">Comparing changes</a><a href="#evidence">Understanding evidence</a>
				</nav>
				<section id="code-explorer">
					<h2>Code explorer</h2>
					<p>Open Code to browse the directory passed to serve --root. No Git repository or architecture configuration is required. Expand folders and files for top-level declarations; expand types for methods and members. Search and filter by symbol kind, visibility and changed status while retaining directory context. Leading icons show symbol kinds and changes: plus for added, minus for removed, pencil for modified and arrow for renamed. Hover an icon to see its meaning, or focus a name with the keyboard for its kind and status. Unchanged items omit the change icon.</p>
					<p>Select an object for its signature, declaration location, available documentation and source. Follow consumer and caller reference sites or inferred structural type relationships. Static calls do not prove runtime execution. Analysis limits and failures remain visible, and files stay readable when declarations cannot be analyzed.</p>
					<p>Optional revision and base fields compare exact Git trees. Object changes include body and documentation changes; removed objects show base-revision source and relationships. Select Compare changes in Code to review in the same workspace. Changes only filters the tree and shows diff hunks; turn it off to see the full file with changes marked. Diffs use one scrollbar, green additions and red deletions, with base/head line numbers. Use the same Compare changes controls for branch review: choose Branch changes, then select Target branch and Review branch from the repository’s branch lists. The current branch is preselected and a known target is suggested; no commit IDs are needed. Local and cached remote branches are listed without fetching. Compare branch tips compares their tips without simulating a merge; Revisions (advanced) retains manual ref entry. Source, comments, history and all review tools share that selection. Both resolved refs and the mode stay visible. Shallow, unrelated or ambiguous histories require an explicit correction rather than guessing a base. Switch between Unified and Side by side layouts, search diff lines, and inspect base or head relationship evidence. Use the Comments, File review, Hotspots, History, Impact and Exports tabs to focus on one part of the comparison. History lists non-merge commits in the selected revision range, independently of the file selected in Source. Path, type and scope filters apply to History and Hotspots; expand a commit to see its affected files. Select a file to open that commit’s changes in Source; a root commit opens its source because it has no parent. Use Add comment for a file comment, or select a base/head line number to comment on that line. Commented lines have a highlighted gutter marker in both diff layouts; select the marker to read, edit or delete the comment. File comments and comments needing reconsideration appear beside the file heading. Saving or deleting keeps the current file, layout, filters and scroll position. Edits preserve the original revision and source anchor; conflicting edits require reopening the comment. Comments are saved locally with their original source context; Download comments (.md) collects the whole comparison into one Markdown document, including findings on other files. Comments on subsequently edited files are marked for reconsideration. Module and seam labels only show declared architecture. File review saves viewed markers and an editable checklist per comparison. Later revisions mark the checklist for reconsideration. Hotspots offer frequency, churn and net-size metrics, sorting, directory/module grouping, path exclusions and a heatmap. Chart links filter commits and hotspot paths; hotspot totals retain the full history range for matching paths. Generated exclusions are path heuristics, and binary line statistics stay unknown. Files added and deleted within the range remain inspectable through their touching commits. Review works without architecture configuration; unavailable architecture facts remain labeled. JSON, Markdown and self-contained HTML exports preserve comparison identity, metric definitions, filter settings and original evidence.</p>
				</section>
				<section id="graph-controls">
					<h2>Graph controls</h2>
					<p>Read the graph from left to right: arrows point from consumers to providers. Each box is a module; each arrow groups references between two modules. The layout places dependencies in columns, keeps cycles together, and bundles connections into shared corridors.</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Hover over a module to highlight its connections, or hover over an arrow to highlight that connection.</li>
						<li>Select a module to focus its neighbors and inspect its seams and evidence. Select an arrow to inspect its references.</li>
						<li>Use Clear focus to return to the wider graph. Drag modules to move them; connections reroute around the boxes. Scroll to zoom and drag the background to pan.</li>
						<li>Search by module, seam, or consumer ID. Violations only limits the graph to current violations. These filters combine with the selected reference mode.</li>
						<li>Outside a comparison, expand a module's seams from its details to inspect their consumers. Copy IDs and agent prompts to refer to the same entities in the CLI or your coding agent.</li>
					</ul>
				</section>
				<section id="reference-modes">
					<h2>Reference modes</h2>
					<p>Use the view selector above the graph to choose a reference mode. All references shows every kind together. These modes filter the kinds of references grouped into the arrows. An arrow can appear in several modes when it contains different kinds of references. Filtered modes hide unconnected modules, except the focused module and changed modules during a comparison.</p>
					<div class="help-table">
						<table>
							<thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">What it shows</th></tr></thead>
							<tbody>
								<tr><th scope="row">Callable references</th><td>References to functions, methods, and function-valued fields. These include function values passed around as well as calls, so they do not prove execution.</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">Data references</th><td>References to types, interfaces, fields, and other non-callable symbols.</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">Imports</th><td>Module or package imports. An import alone does not tell us which symbols or seams are used.</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">Construction wiring</th><td>References to construction-only seams and outgoing dependencies from construction-only modules, such as bootstrap. Construction classification takes priority over callable, data, or import classification.</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">All references</th><td>All of these kinds of dependencies together.</td></tr>
							</tbody>
						</table>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="comparisons">
					<h2>Comparing changes</h2>
					<p>Use the existing Compare changes controls, choose a mode, and select Compare. In Code, branch modes offer Target branch and Review branch dropdowns populated from local and cached remote-tracking branches. The current branch is preselected and a known target is suggested; no commit IDs are needed.</p>
     <div class="help-table">
      <table aria-label="Comparison modes">
       <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">What it shows</th><th scope="col">When to use it</th></tr></thead>
       <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Working changes</th><td>HEAD compared with the final checkout contents, including staged, unstaged and untracked files that Git does not ignore.</td><td>Review work you have not committed yet.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Branch changes</th><td>The review branch tip compared with its shared ancestor with the target branch, called the merge base. Changes made only on the target since that ancestor are excluded.</td><td>Recommended for normal branch review: what work does this branch introduce?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Compare branch tips</th><td>The current contents of the target branch compared directly with the current contents of the review branch. Differences caused by changes on either branch are included.</td><td>Investigate divergence or check whether the branches currently have the same contents.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Revisions (advanced)</th><td>The two committed refs you enter, such as tags, commit IDs or Git revision expressions, compared as exact trees.</td><td>Inspect a specific historical pair when branch selection is not enough.</td></tr>
       </tbody>
      </table>
     </div>
     <h3>Why branch modes can show different changes</h3>
     <p>Suppose your feature branch splits from main, then main adds a new file while you change a function on the feature branch. Branch changes shows your function change and excludes the new file on main. Compare branch tips also shows that file as missing from your branch, even though you never deleted it. Use Branch changes for normal review; use Compare branch tips when you want all current differences between the branches.</p>
     <p>Neither branch mode simulates a merge or predicts merge conflicts. Comparisons do not change your checkout or index. History in both branch modes lists commits on the review branch that are not reachable from the selected target; it is separate from the final source diff. Comments and checklists belong to the selected comparison refs and mode, so changing modes shows separate saved review state.</p>
     <p>Code review works without architecture configuration. To compare the architecture graph, both revisions need a valid seamgraph.yaml.</p>
					<p>The graph includes both snapshots so removed modules and connections remain visible and clickable. Select them to inspect their before state. Run Compare again after further edits; Clear comparison reloads the current architecture.</p>
					<ul>
						<li><strong>+ Added:</strong> green modules and arrows.</li>
						<li><strong>− Removed:</strong> red dashed modules and arrows.</li>
						<li><strong>~ Modified:</strong> amber module badges for changed files, even when public seams and dependencies stay the same.</li>
						<li><strong>Modified connections:</strong> amber dotted arrows when references change. Removing some references while others still connect the same modules modifies the connection rather than removing it.</li>
						<li><strong>Seam changed:</strong> public seam membership or member signatures changed.</li>
						<li><strong>Config changed:</strong> a module definition changed. Project configuration edits are also reported in the comparison results.</li>
						<li><strong>Policy changed:</strong> new or resolved violations affect the module. Current violations retain a separate red border or halo.</li>
					</ul>
					<h3>Changed + neighbors</h3>
					<p>Available after a comparison. It keeps changed modules, both endpoints of changed connections, and their directly connected neighbors for context. It hides the rest while respecting the reference mode, search, focus, and violation filters. If those modules already cover the graph, the view may look the same. Uncheck it to show the full comparison again.</p>
					<p>Changed file paths are attributed using the module roots on each side. Exact-content moves appear as renames; edited moves appear as a deletion and addition. Configuration changes are separate from source changes. These indicators show what changed, not how difficult or risky a change is.</p>
				</section>
				<section id="evidence">
					<h2>Understanding evidence</h2>
					<p>Edges show observed dependencies. A module import alone does not prove use through an interface. Structural interface compatibility is a type fact, rather than an observed consumer dependency.</p>
					<p>Source evidence records where a reference was found with confidence marked exact or best-effort and resolution marked resolved, unresolved, or dynamic. Missing or uncertain evidence does not establish that a dependency is absent. Analysis errors and incomplete results need investigation before treating the graph as complete.</p>
					<p>Contract verification covers explicitly declared requirements using current linked test evidence. Coverage can show which members were exercised; missing coverage is unknown rather than proof that a member was never called. Passing verification does not prove the absence of bugs.</p>
					<p>Rule proposals show a preview. Only explicit confirmation writes the project configuration, with a backup and a check for concurrent changes.</p>
				</section>
			</article>
		</main>
	}
}
